dafdef:Property_n_4392A7456668475094557CEF42943004
    a dafm:PropertyDefinition ;
    rdfs:label "CIDR" ;
    dafm:eaGuid "{4392A745-6668-4750-9455-7CEF42943004}" ;
    dafm:sourceDatatype "String" ;
    dafm:typingStatus dafm:ExplicitPrimitiveType ;
    dafm:isDerived false ;
    dafm:projectionPredicate dafp:dIPRange__CIDR ;
    rdfs:comment "The term CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing) range refers to an IP address range that is defined by a combination of an IP address and a routing prefix (or subnet mask). CIDR notation is used to specify IP address ranges in a compact format, which is widely used in network configuration, including the configuration of cloud resources, subnets, or containers.\n\nCIDR range is written as an IP address followed by a slash (\"/\") and a number. For example, 192.168.0.0/24 refers to an IP range where the first 24 bits are network bits, and the remaining bits (in this case, 8 bits) are used for defining host addresses. This notation helps specify networks of varying sizes and allows more flexible allocation of IP addresses compared to the traditional class-based method." ;
    dafm:minimumCardinality "1" ;
    dafm:maximumCardinality "1" .

daf:dIPRange dafm:hasPropertyDefinition dafdef:Property_n_4392A7456668475094557CEF42943004 .

dafp:dIPRange__CIDR
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:label "CIDR" ;
    rdfs:domain daf:dIPRange ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:comment "The term CIDR (Classless Inter-Domain Routing) range refers to an IP address range that is defined by a combination of an IP address and a routing prefix (or subnet mask). CIDR notation is used to specify IP address ranges in a compact format, which is widely used in network configuration, including the configuration of cloud resources, subnets, or containers.\n\nCIDR range is written as an IP address followed by a slash (\"/\") and a number. For example, 192.168.0.0/24 refers to an IP range where the first 24 bits are network bits, and the remaining bits (in this case, 8 bits) are used for defining host addresses. This notation helps specify networks of varying sizes and allows more flexible allocation of IP addresses compared to the traditional class-based method." .

